### The constructions

**One family of objects.** Works identified by their content; references between them that carry credit; events on clocks. Every experience below is these three with a permission set.

**The room.** Shared: the work (the same stems, by content), the clock (a beat grid; playing together needs alignment within tens of milliseconds, solved at room scale by tempo-and-phase synchronization), and each person's events (what they did, never the audio). Each device renders the whole room from the shared work, the shared clock and everyone's events. No master mix, no host; a late joiner takes the event history; a dropped device leaves the room playing. Who may send events that change the mix decides the experience: one person (a radio station, an artist's station, a listening party), everyone on their own layer (a band). A contribution is a credited layer: the talent bar at zero.

**The wave.** A surf references the song by content and the surf it came from; the wave is the tree those references form. Full credit without decay is the correct rule: everyone on the path from the root to a listener is a necessary cause of that arrival. Credit is reachability. The interface in which each node reveals everything it is connected to is the same fact shown: the connections are the data.

**The work and its making.** In the DAW, immutable content-addressed objects give the full edit history at the cost of the changes only. Producers wire their own flows from typed parts, checked as they are joined. A synthesizer is a small system of differential equations: a patch is a typed program, recomposable, provable, shared like a stem.

**The station and the tip.** The station plays one song to everyone at one moment, with the listeners present to each other. A tip is an event on the room's clock: it lands at the moment of the music it answers, so the artist sees what moved whom. Tipping is the business model Crowdsurf lacked; the wave says who brought each tipper.

**The evaluation loop.** Dynamic time warping returns a warping path, a monotone correspondence between performance and score: identity as a path with its witness, carrying where the player rushed, dragged or dropped. A piano battle is two performances whose paths anyone can check.

**Papers and comments.** Reading a paper is a surf of the paper; a lab's reading graph is a wave; notes attach to exact spans. A reply references the claim it answers, not the message before it.

### What follows

**The sample graph and the wave are one credit structure in two directions.** The sample graph credits backward in time: what a work is built from. The wave credits outward through people: who carried it to whom. A work's complete credit is both. A tip routed down the sample graph reaches, from a fan's love for "Through the Wire", Chaka Khan and the writers of "Through the Fire" in the same moment. That is "the future serves the past just as the past serves the future" as a payment rule, and it answers Gregory Coleman, the drummer of the Amen break, who was never paid for the most sampled drum break in history: the break is paid every time anyone loves anything built on it. Every one of the 216 sources in Ye's catalogue is credited in `docs/ye-every-record-credited.md`.

**Credit transports along paths.** A live take relates to the studio work by a witnessed path (the warping path plus the layers added). Because identity is a path and not a filename, whatever is owed to the work is owed along the path to the performance and back. A remix, a cover and a live version are the work up to a path, credited as such.

**There is no artist/fan duality because a surf and a layer are the same kind of object.** Both are a reference with credit: one adds a carrier, the other adds a sound. The first person to surf an unknown artist is credited, by the no-decay rule, for everyone who comes after: the A&R role of a label, held by everyone and paid to whoever had the taste first. Talent is revealed by which layers rooms keep and which surfs spread, a measurement no gatekeeper holds. That is decentralized tastemaking made exact.


### A night in a room

A producer opens a room for a new track. The stems are content-addressed: anyone joining fetches the same objects by their identity, and the room's clock is a beat grid every device follows. Two hundred people join. Each device renders the whole room from the shared work, the shared clock and everyone's events; nobody streams audio to anybody.

The producer holds the mix, so it is a listening party. Midway she opens a layer to everyone: now it is a band. A teenager in another country adds a bassline as a layer. It is a credited layer, an object referencing the work, and the room keeps it: other people's devices keep rendering it, which is the measurement of talent no gatekeeper holds. A listener tips at the moment the bassline drops; the tip is an event on the room's clock, so the producer and the teenager both see what moved whom.

Someone surfs the track to their friends. Everyone who comes to it through them is in their wave, and full credit without decay means every tip from that wave credits everyone on the path from the root. The track samples a 1970s soul record; the tip, routed down the sample graph, reaches its writers in the same moment.

The next morning a pianist uploads a live take of the hook. Dynamic time warping returns the warping path between her performance and the stems: the witness that it is the same work, with where she rushed and dragged. Credit transports along that path. Her take is the work up to a path, and is credited as such.

Nothing in the night needed a label, a platform or a distributor. Every relation (layer, surf, sample, take, tip) is a reference with credit, held by the people who made it.

