## 35. Neural co-processing

"now I want you to go extremely deep into the field of neural co-processing which is what I would've done my masters in but have instead done a Magnum opus masterwork on" (Avik, 2026-11-17). He left that master's on ethical grounds: the work was early and theoretical, but the literature it rested on was primate sacrifice, and taking part would have meant accepting that. He would rather humans accept death than kill to prolong life.

The field: a brain-computer interface decodes (reads neural activity into a control signal); a computer-brain interface encodes (writes into the brain by stimulation); a co-processor does both in one device, closing the loop, both it and the nervous system optimizing one shared cost (Rao, 2019). The Berkeley lineage around Jose Carmena calls this a two-learner system: the brain and the decoder each adapting to the other. Its engineers went on to found Neuralink (Dongjin Seo, neural dust). Motor-cortex activity lies on a low-dimensional manifold, and decoders that stay within it are learned quickly while those outside it are not (Sadtler et al., *Nature*, 2014): the brain reassociates patterns it already produces rather than making new ones. That has the shape of the compression theorems in the math repository: a decoder within the manifold asks the brain only to transport patterns it already has along a new map; reassociation is transport, and the cost falls on what the repertoire does not already contain.

The workflow node `brain-machine-interfaces`: Meta's TRIBE (2025) predicts whole-brain fMRI responses to video, audio and text from model embeddings; TRIBE v2 (2026), over 1,000 hours of fMRI across 720 subjects, which Meta calls a "digital twin of human neural activity", predicts responses of unseen subjects to novel stimuli. An encoding model with that reach lets stimuli be searched for the response they drive, the method neuroscience already published on animals (images synthesized to drive chosen neurons to their maximum). Engagement maximization by neural prediction: "Facebook has brain research on which video frames produce max neural activity for engagement-maxxing, this connects to everything, from evil tech information warfare and neuralink elon neuroscience".

Hyperactivity's answer is the receptive interface the engagement interface inverts. Eye contact is co-processing at the speed of light (Avik's essay, part 7 of his Information Dynamic series): two nervous systems coupled through light, each retina brain tissue sampling the light field, both observing and observed, a feedback loop at near-minimum physical latency, the highest-bandwidth, lowest-latency channel between spatially separated nervous systems maintaining distinct boundaries. The human interface is built to be that: reader and store each the other's observer, the interface reading what the person dwells on, skips and holds as data co-processed with them, toward the person's own intention and never toward a population norm.

The market is the brain-computer interface industry, the attention economy it serves, and the advertising it funds. The engagement interface sells the person's attention to the highest bidder; the receptive interface serves the person. A technology that co-processes with a person instead of optimizing them for engagement removes the reason advertising pays for neural prediction.

