# Questions a reader asks, answered from the work

**If everything is free, where does ten trillion dollars come from?** From the rents the work removes, reaching the work through four channels its own account of markets names: exposure (positions on the repricings, held before the information is public), forcing the outcome (a portfolio of initiatives handed the construction), the first firm built as one program (a share of the concentration), and the credit root (full credit, no decay, for everything reachable). Chapter 39 onward.

**Isn't a language model the intelligence, and this a programming language?** The aim of hyperactivity is not a programming language. It is the proper form of the internet and of global computation. The intelligence a model approximates statistically (recall of what followed similar contexts) is exact transport over an exact index that fits in linear space; and the theory that says what a model means also says exactly where its meaning ends: where seeing selected and text could not. Chapters 17–19.

**Why would a firm rewrite its code?** It does not. A language is a value of the host, so existing code is lifted with its meaning kept, specifications attach to the exact source object, and proofs erase back to the unchanged code. The firm keeps its C++ or its OCaml and gains proofs, specialization and end-to-end optimization. Chapters 8 and 28.

**Why would anyone trust code an agent wrote?** Because trust stops depending on the author (P11). A claim and its justification are one construction the machine checks, so a result is as trustworthy as the check, whoever or whatever produced it. That is what lets ten thousand artificial researchers work in a firm (C10). Chapter 28.

**Without a canonical state, how does anything agree?** The pool grows by union, and union is proven commutative, associative and idempotent, so every participant's view converges whatever order they exchange in. Disagreement between people is two constructions over shared premises, shown with the point where inference parts. Chapters 22–23.

**Doesn't conserving credit need a ledger?** The invariant is proved over a specified global state no participant observes; allocation and delivery preserve the total by cancellation, with no global scheduler. Each participant knows exactly what its own view warrants about the rest (the fiber). The protocol under it (message identity, idempotent receipt) is stated in the paper as what a real protocol defines, and it is an open construction. Chapters 13 and Part Eight.

**Why medicine first among the sciences?** Because its central problem is identity, and identity is the work's first construction. The field's own records state the defect (26.25% tautomer resolution, no standard canonical SMILES, Eroom's law, the collapse of predictive validity), and the construction that resolves it is written and checks. Chapter 30.

**Why music, if the market is $31.7 billion?** Because it is the front with the widest reach to the people who adopt; because the wave and the sample graph are the credit structure of the whole economy in its most legible form; and because the $31.7 billion is the floor of what moves when tipping, A&R, sampling credit and teaching move into rooms. Chapter 29.

**Why would the local device beat the data centre?** Because what the data centre sells is mostly recomputation and location. A result derived once is a lookup anywhere it is held; a construction received by content is as good from a neighbour as from a server; and a process that needs only the memory its local operation on the graph needs runs on the device in hand. What remains for large machines is work that has never been derived, and that work is shared once it is. Chapters 12, 14 and 25.

**How do artists get paid before credit is the allocator?** In dollars, routed by credit. A tip is an event on the room's clock, paid in money today; the wave and the sample graph say who it is owed to, along every path, in full. Credit decides the routing now and becomes the allocator as what it allocates stops being rationed by price. Chapters 13 and 29.

**What happens to the people whose work the middle employs?** The talent bar goes to zero, because that is how talent is revealed. Research capacity becomes a compute budget, and the people in it become authors of specializations and constructions that apply everywhere at once. Knowledge belongs to the work, not to the gatekeeper, and onboarding is a walk. A society that does not need to ration information does not need people to ration it. Chapters 28, 29 and 32.

**Does proven code survive real hardware?** The refinement theorem relates the bytes to the source: compiled behavior is behavior the source permits, under the stated machine semantics, which include the memory model and concurrency as relations among events. The paper carries it through a stack machine and through the x86 bytes of a C++ frame decoder. What is assumed is stated as a premise, and a premise can be checked. Chapters 10 and 11.

**Why Ye?** Because his whole public career is the argument that the artist, not the label, owns the work; because his catalogue is the densest sample graph in popular music; because he already shipped the stem player and released an album on it; and because he reaches the people who adopt. The opportunity put to him is aligned with what he has fought for: music with no middle, credited through what it is built from and who carried it. Chapter 29.

**Why is open source not giving the value away?** Because the value was never in holding the code. It is in the four captures of Part Six, and every tool given away is a channel that brings a community into the one graph where those captures are made. Everything is free, and that is what maximizes value creation. Chapters 39 and 44.

**What makes this one person's to launch?** The work is one organism, and it was built by one person directing agents, which is the rationale the heads of OpenAI and Anthropic give for expecting a billion-dollar company run by one person. The launch is the catalyst of the repricing, and the launch is his.

# Part Eight. Open constructions

Each construction below, once built, strengthens the case. Where the repository states the work it needs, that is given.

## The economic constructions

**Issuance.** Conservation says credit is preserved under allocation and delivery; it does not say how credit enters. The wave is a candidate issuance rule: credit enters when a work is made and flows by reachability, the sample graph backward in time and the wave outward through people. A work's complete credit is both. Stated as a construction, issuance is a transition that creates credit at a work and a theorem relating the total to the works made, and the money statement of chapter 15 becomes a statement about a currency with a defined supply.

**The protocol under the invariant.** §10.3 proves conservation of the specified state and states what a real protocol defines: message identities, duplication resistance, receipt acknowledgment, failure, reallocation, and the conditions under which credit returns. If messages may be duplicated without an idempotency rule, the theorem fails. The protocol, written with its transition rules and proved to refine the invariant (by handler composition and safety transfer, §10), is what makes credit an allocator between strangers.

**Who.** The identity of a person as a fiber of witnessed relations: what makes two records the same person. It is the same construction as chemical identity (three identities kept apart, each with its evidence) and as the warping path that witnesses two performances are the same work. With it, credit and trust attach to people without an account held by a provider.

**The long legs.** Exposure is captured whole when the positions include what reprices up as well as what reprices down: the assets that gain when recomputation, the middle and the dollar as allocator reprice. Each is identified by the same analysis that identifies the shorts: what does the current price reflect, and does it already reflect the exposure of this information (Q5.1).

**The first firm.** The presentation opens with the Citadel Securities superorganism and the Jane Street superorganism. The exposition to Yaron Minsky is written, in OCaml's terms.

**The entity.** Hyperactive Labs as a California public benefit corporation recognized under 501(c)(3), holding the commons; the positions, the portfolio and the firm partnership beside it; Unison Computing's relation to Hyperactive Labs as the model for companies building on the commons.

**The artists Ye should meet.** Their names and one link each, for the brief to him.

## The product constructions

**Scope by key.** The server's pool grows by proven union; what is open is visibility decided where an object crosses a boundary, with a portable key only its audience can compute, so restricted content keeps its semantic identity and gives up only being shared with identical objects outside its audience.

**Memory is local, everywhere.** No process holds the whole of anything: the site's corpus served one work at a time, text held as a tree of content-addressed pieces with List Word as specification and the packed buffer as storage, and every process's cost derived from its definitions.

**The human interface.** Built: the reader, the scene, live presence, scrubbing through time, the morph between equivalent statements, the field drawn by one shader. Its processes continue: the continuous zoom from the root goal to a word's root, the blame graph drawn edge by edge, repetition shown as light from the exact index, the onboarding walk as a camera path, one room in extended reality with each lineage at its place.

